CALL newPutForth(64, 1, 'Fluoridated water has reduced tooth decay by 25% in the U.S. since the 1960s — across all income levels. One of the cheapest, most effective public health interventions ever devised. Not controversial.');
CALL newPutForth(336, 1, 'The correlation is real and consistent — fluoridated communities have measurably better dental health outcomes. This isn\'t a hypothesis; it\'s documented in studies spanning fifty years across dozens of countries.');
CALL newPutForth(337, 1, 'At the levels approved for drinking water — 0.7 mg/L — no credible study has found cognitive effects. The anti-fluoride panic is based on research from regions with naturally occurring fluoride at 4x that level.');
CALL newPutForth(338, 1, 'Local and state health officials have reviewed decades of evidence and broadly agree that 0.7 to 1.5 mg per liter is safe and effective. That\'s the scientific consensus, not a pharmaceutical conspiracy.');
CALL newPutForth(339, 1, 'This is the expected outcome — better dental health improves nutrition, reduces systemic infections, and reduces healthcare costs. The economic argument for fluoridation is as strong as the public health one.');
CALL newPutForth(340, 1, 'Every time a community defluoridates its water, decay rates go up — especially in children. Fighting fluoridation isn\'t protecting public health; it\'s harming it. This fight should stay lost.');
CALL newPutForth(416, 1, 'Science gives us the best available picture of reality. That\'s the tool we have. When scientific consensus is clear — vaccines work, fluoride is safe, climate data exists — we should respect that consensus.');
CALL newPutForth(418, 1, 'If you want to sell a remedy, prove it works in a properly controlled trial. That\'s not a high bar — it\'s the minimum bar. If you can\'t meet it, you\'re not selling medicine; you\'re selling hope.');
CALL newPutForth(419, 1, 'Making health claims you can\'t substantiate — or that a reasonable person would understand to be unproven — is fraud. The supplement industry runs on this. It should be regulated as fraud.');
CALL newPutForth(426, 1, 'No credible scientific body has established a link between vaccines and autism. The original Wakefield study was fraudulent — its author lost his medical license. The claim has been retested thousands of times.');
CALL newPutForth(427, 1, 'Vaccines are among the most rigorously tested medical interventions in history. The childhood vaccine schedule has been studied for decades. The benefits are overwhelming. The safety record is established.');
CALL newPutForth(735, 1, 'When people travel freely across borders and trade flows globally, infectious disease follows. Vaccines protect not just individuals but supply chains, factories, and the economy. This is practical, not ideological.');
CALL newPutForth(895, 1, 'Healthcare system in the U.S. needs work — costs are too high, VA is underfunded, rural access is a problem. Reform is needed. But the answer is more market competition and less government bureaucracy, not Medicare for All.');
CALL newPutForth(897, 1, 'The U.S. spends 17% of GDP on healthcare — more than any other developed country. Much of that is insurance overhead, administrative waste, and defensive medicine driven by lawsuit exposure. It\'s not efficient.');
CALL newPutForth(899, 1, 'The current system is neither fully market-based nor fully government-controlled — it\'s the worst of both. Insurance companies and hospital systems have no real competition. That\'s the inefficiency. Fix the market.');
CALL newPutForth(909, 1, 'Employer-based health insurance was a World War II accident — companies used benefits to compete for workers under wartime wage controls. There\'s no good reason to tie healthcare to your job in 2024.');
CALL newPutForth(910, 1, 'Government-run healthcare adds layers of bureaucracy without the efficiency pressure of competition. The DMV doesn\'t run itself well; why would we expect a government-run hospital network to be different?');
CALL newPutForth(911, 1, 'Private enterprise operates under competitive pressure to reduce costs and improve outcomes. Government healthcare removes both incentives. Private control — with proper regulation against abuse — is the right model.');
CALL newPutForth(912, 1, 'The VA has some of the worst wait times and administrative failures in American healthcare. Veterans served this country and are getting a system built for bureaucrats, not patients. Fix it or privatize it.');
CALL newPutForth(913, 1, 'Veterans put their bodies on the line for this country. They deserve the best healthcare we can offer — not the leftovers of a government bureaucracy that can\'t be fired for failure.');
CALL newPutForth(914, 1, 'Private health insurance is also a bureaucratic nightmare — prior authorizations, denied claims, narrow networks. The current system gives you the worst of both worlds: private greed and government inflexibility.');
CALL newPutForth(916, 1, 'Veterans gave up years of their lives, their health, and sometimes their families for this country. What they get in return — underfunded VA, inadequate mental health services — is a national disgrace.');
CALL newPutForth(917, 1, 'The VA varies dramatically — some facilities are genuinely excellent; others are unconscionable failures. It\'s not monolithic. The solution is accountability and competition, not universal defense or universal condemnation.');
CALL newPutForth(920, 1, 'Head Start, DARE, ethanol mandates, Cash for Clunkers — the government\'s program hall of shame. Studies proved they don\'t work. Kept running them anyway because someone\'s job depended on it.');
CALL newPutForth(922, 1, 'Medicare and Medicaid should be privatized through a voucher system — let beneficiaries choose their insurers. Competition drives better service and lower costs. The current government-run system has no incentive for either.');




